## Removing<span pulumi-lang-nodejs=" sbercloud.VpcPeeringConnectionAccepter " pulumi-lang-dotnet=" sbercloud.VpcPeeringConnectionAccepter " pulumi-lang-go=" VpcPeeringConnectionAccepter " pulumi-lang-python=" VpcPeeringConnectionAccepter " pulumi-lang-yaml=" sbercloud.VpcPeeringConnectionAccepter " pulumi-lang-java=" sbercloud.VpcPeeringConnectionAccepter " pulumi-lang-hcl=" sbercloud_vpc_peering_connection_accepter "> sbercloud.VpcPeeringConnectionAccepter </span>from your configuration

SberCloud allows a cross-tenant VPC Peering Connection to be deleted from either the requester's or accepter's side.
However, Terraform only allows the VPC Peering Connection to be deleted from the requester's side by removing the
corresponding <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`sbercloud.VpcPeeringConnection`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`sbercloud.VpcPeeringConnection`" pulumi-lang-go="`VpcPeeringConnection`" pulumi-lang-python="`VpcPeeringConnection`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`sbercloud.VpcPeeringConnection`" pulumi-lang-java="`sbercloud.VpcPeeringConnection`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`sbercloud_vpc_peering_connection`">`sbercloud.VpcPeeringConnection`</span> resource from your configuration.
Removing a <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`sbercloud.VpcPeeringConnectionAccepter`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`sbercloud.VpcPeeringConnectionAccepter`" pulumi-lang-go="`VpcPeeringConnectionAccepter`" pulumi-lang-python="`VpcPeeringConnectionAccepter`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`sbercloud.VpcPeeringConnectionAccepter`" pulumi-lang-java="`sbercloud.VpcPeeringConnectionAccepter`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`sbercloud_vpc_peering_connection_accepter`">`sbercloud.VpcPeeringConnectionAccepter`</span> resource from your configuration will remove it from your
state file and management, but will not destroy the VPC Peering Connection.

